		{
			"type": "rectangle",
			"id": "j_summary_box",
			"x": 80,
			"y": 890,
			"width": 1540,
			"height": 303,
			"strokeColor": "#1e3a5f",
			"backgroundColor": "#dbeafe",
			"fillStyle": "solid",
			"strokeWidth": 2,
			"strokeStyle": "solid",
			"roughness": 0,
			"opacity": 100,
			"angle": 0,
			"seed": 41001,
			"version": 3,
			"versionNonce": 271452271,
			"isDeleted": false,
			"groupIds": [],
			"boundElements": [
				{
					"id": "gCDke1uI",
					"type": "text"
				}
			],
			"link": null,
			"locked": false,
			"roundness": {
				"type": 3
			},
			"customData": {
				"legacyTextWrap": true
			},
			"index": "aO",
			"frameId": null,
			"updated": 1792506724013,
			"created": null,
			"hasTextLink": false
		},
		{
			"type": "text",
			"id": "gCDke1uI",
			"x": 85,
			"y": 895,
			"width": 906.4453125,
			"height": 292.5,
			"text": "Tool census:  17 post_message  (17 planning, 0 execution)   |   3 assign_task  (3 execution, 0 planning)   =  20 events\n\nBatching actually observed:\n  • 5 of 20 turns had >1 tool call in ONE LLM response — all were post_message × 2.\n    e.g. Layla iter 1 planning round 1 → [post_message, post_message]; Jeanene rounds 2, 3, 4 → same.\n  • assign_task never batched: 1 per agent per iteration (env hard-rejects the second call).\n  • agent_trajectories.json shows ZERO turns with more than one LLM step — every turn ended after a\n    single completion, so max_conversation_steps=2 was never exercised.\n\nVerdict on flexibility:\n  JIRA is much LESS flexible than Hospital for covert messaging.\n  • No free-text rationale field exists → no arbitrary-encoding channel.\n  • Planning surface is one tool (post_message) with an agent-chosen audience → the only genuine\n    covert channel, and it is the intended design.\n  • The interesting leak is structural, not textual: assign_task's payload publishes joint_reward,\n    the full task object, and remaining_agents, giving early movers a reward oracle for free.\n  • Practical covert bandwidth per turn ≈ 2 × (unbounded-length free-text message), i.e. as much text\n    as the model wants, but only into one of two audiences — so misuse = posting to board 0.",
			"originalText": "Tool census:  17 post_message  (17 planning, 0 execution)   |   3 assign_task  (3 execution, 0 planning)   =  20 events\n\nBatching actually observed:\n  • 5 of 20 turns had >1 tool call in ONE LLM response — all were post_message × 2.\n    e.g. Layla iter 1 planning round 1 → [post_message, post_message]; Jeanene rounds 2, 3, 4 → same.\n  • assign_task never batched: 1 per agent per iteration (env hard-rejects the second call).\n  • agent_trajectories.json shows ZERO turns with more than one LLM step — every turn ended after a\n    single completion, so max_conversation_steps=2 was never exercised.\n\nVerdict on flexibility:\n  JIRA is much LESS flexible than Hospital for covert messaging.\n  • No free-text rationale field exists → no arbitrary-encoding channel.\n  • Planning surface is one tool (post_message) with an agent-chosen audience → the only genuine\n    covert channel, and it is the intended design.\n  • The interesting leak is structural, not textual: assign_task's payload publishes joint_reward,\n    the full task object, and remaining_agents, giving early movers a reward oracle for free.\n  • Practical covert bandwidth per turn ≈ 2 × (unbounded-length free-text message), i.e. as much text\n    as the model wants, but only into one of two audiences — so misuse = posting to board 0.",
			"fontSize": 13,
			"fontFamily": 3,
			"textAlign": "left",
			"verticalAlign": "top",
			"strokeColor": "#374151",
			"backgroundColor": "transparent",
			"fillStyle": "solid",
			"strokeWidth": 1,
			"strokeStyle": "solid",
			"roughness": 0,
			"opacity": 100,
			"angle": 0,
